#
# Counter-example: a boundary math:CellIncidence and its declared transpose coboundary
# math:CellIncidence (linked by math:adjointIncidence) name the SAME two cells with their
# incidenceCoface/incidenceFace endpoints swapped — a TRUE transpose entry, per
# math:adjointIncidence's own definition ("the two endpoints swapped and the sign
# preserved") — but carry DISAGREEING ±1 signs (+1 vs −1), so the coboundary matrix is
# not the transpose of the boundary matrix and the adjunction δ = ∂* fails. It fires
# math:BoundaryCoboundaryAdjunctionConstraint (shacl.SPARQLConstraintComponent →
# math:BrokenBoundaryCoboundaryAdjunction) and NO other finding: both incidences are
# complete (coface, face, sign), and no math:incidentTo relation is asserted between the
# two cells, so no codimension-2 boundary composition the general-sum constraint would
# score can form.

@prefix math: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/math/> .
@prefix ex:   <http://example.org/math/adjunction-inconsistent/> .

ex:cX a math:Cell ; math:cellDimension ex:dim2 .
ex:cY a math:Cell ; math:cellDimension ex:dim1 .
ex:dim1 a math:NaturalNumber ; math:inNumberSystem math:naturalNumbers ; math:isExact true .
ex:dim2 a math:NaturalNumber ; math:inNumberSystem math:naturalNumbers ; math:isExact true .

# The boundary entry [∂cX : cY] = +1 and its declared adjoint coboundary entry [δcY:cX] —
# the SAME two cells, endpoints swapped — whose transpose sign should also be +1 but is
# authored as −1: a genuine transpose pair with a broken adjunction.
ex:iBoundary a math:CellIncidence ;
    math:incidenceCoface ex:cX ; math:incidenceFace ex:cY ; math:incidenceSign 1 ;
    math:adjointIncidence ex:iCoboundary .
ex:iCoboundary a math:CellIncidence ;
    math:incidenceCoface ex:cY ; math:incidenceFace ex:cX ; math:incidenceSign -1 .
